        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Talmud</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 200 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">The rabbis of the Land of Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>: the Tannaim, who produced the Mishnah in the second century CE, and the Amoraim of the third to fifth centuries, with later editors called Savoraim; tradition names Rav Ashi and Ravina II as compilers of the Babylonian Talmud</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A commentary of 63 tractates in six orders, written largely in Jewish Babylonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> with Mishnaic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biblical-hebrew">Biblical Hebrew</a> quotations; the Babylonian Talmud has about 2.5 million words and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilna</a> edition 2,711 folio pages</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">The only complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> Codex Hebraica 95 (1342); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> Genizah fragments; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, Firenze and Vatican codices; copies in study houses worldwide</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Mishnah and Gemara together; the back and forth of rabbinic debate; the Oral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> as Jewish tradition holds it; law (halakha) alongside legend (aggadah); the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilna</a> page with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashi">Rashi</a> and Tosafot; a long history of commentary; the Disputation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and the burnings of its copies</span></div>
