    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Talmud is the Mishnah together with the Gemara, the rabbinic commentary on it; the Babylonian Talmud is the one usually meant, and in Rabbinic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> it ranks second in authority only to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>.</li><li>It records the disagreements of thousands of rabbis rather than only their conclusions, and it mixes law with legend, ethics, history and folklore.</li><li>Its history outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> includes disputations, censorship and burnings of its copies, and also close study by Christian scholars and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> that became the model for later editions.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Because the Talmud's page was set in print by Daniel Bomberg in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> between 1520 and 1523, almost every <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> since has followed the same pagination, and a reader today still cites a passage by its folio, such as Berachot 23b.</p></aside>
    </section>

