    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">70 CE</span><span class="rf-text">The destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a>, from which Talmudic tradition is dated as emerging</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2nd century</span><span class="rf-text">The Tannaim produce the Mishnah, the framework on which the Talmud is built</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">Late 4th or early 5th century</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> Talmud is compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">500 CE</span><span class="rf-text">Ravina II, by medieval chronicles' reckoning, dies, and the Talmud is said to have stopped</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">6th century</span><span class="rf-text">Modern scholars think the Babylonian Talmud was most likely completed, after a last redaction by the Savoraim</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1240</span><span class="rf-text">The Disputation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> examines charges against the Talmud brought by Nicholas Donin</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1520</span><span class="rf-text">Daniel Bomberg begins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> the first complete Babylonian Talmud at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1835</span><span class="rf-text">Menachem Romm of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilna</a> prints the edition on which most later ones are modelled</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/torah"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/torah.jpg" alt="Torah" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Тора._Нацыянальная_бібліятэка_Беларусі.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Talmud is a central text of Rabbinic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> and a primary source of Jewish law (halakha) and theology. Talmudic tradition emerged and was compiled between the destruction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Second Temple</a> in 70 CE and the Arab conquest in the early seventh century. It rests on the Oral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orthodox-judaism">Orthodox Judaism</a> maintains was revealed in some form together with the written Torah.</p><p>Its first layer is the Mishnah, a compilation of legal opinions and debates by the rabbis known as the Tannaim, teachers of the second century CE. The rabbis of the third to fifth centuries, the Amoraim, then produced the two Talmuds by analysing and debating the Mishnah. In antiquity there were two major centres of such scholarship, the academies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria-palaestina">Syria Palaestina</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, and a Talmud was compiled in each. Among the Babylonian academies were Nehardea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nisibis">Nisibis</a>, Mahoza, Pumbedita and Sura. The Amoraim began to use <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, then the spoken vernacular, in their discussions around the year 200. Recent scholarship has argued at length that the Babylonian Talmud is also a product of Sasanian culture, drawing on Greek-Roman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-persian">Middle Persian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> sources, and that its contents likely trace to that setting whatever the date of its final redaction.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="content">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What It Is</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hebrew"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hebrew.jpg" alt="Hebrew" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_QIsa_example_of_damage_col_12-13.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jb344tul, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Talmud follows the structure of the Mishnah. It is divided into six orders, known as Shisha Sedarim or by the abbreviation Shas, and these into 63 tractates (masekhtot), each on a more focused subject, with 517 chapters in all. Not every tractate has a Gemara, and the Babylonian commentary covers 37 of the Mishnah's 63.</p><p>The Gemara is broadly a commentary on the Mishnah, arising from a long practice of rabbis analysing and debating it, called shakla v'tarya, 'taking and throwing'. Each discussion is an edited passage called a sugya, often framed as an exchange between a questioner (makshan) and an answerer (tartzan). Much of it is legal, and it seeks the biblical basis for a Mishnaic rule and the reasoning that links the two. It also holds narratives, sermons and sayings, called aggadah, which make up about a third of the Babylonian Talmud and a sixth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. It cites baraitot, teachings of the Oral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> that did not enter the Mishnah, and it records opinions that were not edited out. The work is largely in Jewish Babylonian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a>, with quotations of the Mishnah, baraitot and Tanakh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Talmud&#8221; cites where it covers Mishnah, Gemara:</p><ul><li>Jeffrey L. Rubenstein, Talmudic stories: narrative art, composition, and culture (Johns Hopkins University Press, 1999)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="making">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Was Made</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Tradition holds that the foundations of the Babylonian analysis were laid by Abba Arika (175 to 247), a disciple of Judah ha-Nasi, and ascribes the compilation to two sages, Rav Ashi, president of the Sura Academy from 375 to 427, and Ravina II. The latest date traditionally given is 475, the year Ravina II died. Medieval sources vary: several chronicles put his death in 500 CE, Abraham ibn Daud gives 510, and Joseph ibn Tzaddik 505.</p><p>Even those who hold traditional views accept that the Savoraim made a final redaction in the sixth century. Historians have proposed a wide range of dates but think it most likely finished in the sixth century, or before the Muslim conquests of the mid-seventh at the latest, since it lacks Arabic loanwords and syntax. It must post-date the early fifth century, as it relies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> Talmud, which was compiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, probably between the late fourth century and the first half of the fifth.</p><p>Modern scholars such as David Weiss Halivni and Shamma Friedman separate the Amoraic statements, usually brief, from the longer dialectic of the later Stammaitic authors. Rabbinic tradition holds that the sages cited in the Talmuds did not themselves write it, and that their teachings were edited into rough form around 450 CE (Yerushalmi) and 550 CE (Bavli).</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="reception">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Reception</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews.jpg" alt="Ashkenazi Jews" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi Jews</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maurycy_Gottlieb_-_Jews_Praying_in_the_Synagogue_on_Yom_Kippur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maurycy Gottlieb, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From its completion the Talmud became integral to Jewish scholarship, and a maxim in Pirkei Avot advocates its study from the age of 15. Adin Steinsaltz writes that no other work has had a comparable influence on the theory and practice of Jewish life. Until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haskalah">Haskalah</a> of the 18th and 19th centuries it was the centrepiece of Jewish culture in nearly all communities. Medieval commentary included the surviving full commentary of Chananel ben Chushiel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashi">Rashi</a>'s classic commentary, and the Tosafot, collected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkenazi-jews">Ashkenazi</a> rabbis to explain contradictory statements. Isaac Alfasi (1013 to 1103) extracted binding legal rulings. In the 15th and 16th centuries the intensive method of pilpul arose, and rabbis such as Judah Loew ben Bezalel criticised it. In the late 19th century Hayyim Soloveitchik developed the Brisker method.</p><p>Outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> the response was divided. The Disputation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> of 1240 followed charges made by Nicholas Donin, a convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a>, and led to the first burning of copies in Paris in 1242. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation of 1,922 passages, the Extractiones de Talmud, was made in 1244 and 1245. Some Christian scholars, among them Johann Reuchlin and Johannes Buxtorf, studied it sympathetically.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Talmud&#8221; cites where it covers Pilpul, Contemporary scholarship, Soncino, Other English translations:</p><ul><li>Shai Secunda, The Iranian Talmud: Reading the Bavli in Its Sasanian Context (University of Pennsylvania Press, 2013)</li><li>Marvin J. Heller, Printing the Talmud: a history of the individual treatises printed from 1700 to 1750 (Brill Publishers, 1999)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="survival">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Survival &amp; Rediscovery</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vatican-library.jpg" alt="Vatican Library" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Melozzo_da_Forl%C3%AC_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Melozzo da Forlì, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The only complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> Codex Hebraica 95, from 1342. The earliest fragments, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> Genizah, date from the late seventh or eighth century. Ms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> 2673 (1123) is the earliest Talmudic manuscript with a precisely known date, and Ms. Firenze 7 (1177) the earliest such with complete tractates.</p><p>Copies were burned and censored. After Donin's charges, copies were condemned and burned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1242. A commission of Dominicans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> ordered passages cancelled in 1264. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> copies confiscated by the Inquisition were burned in the Campo de' Fiori on 9 September 1553, and a papal bull of 1554 introduced censorship. In 1564 Pius IV lifted the prohibition but required censorship, and the work could not be titled Talmud, hence the name Shas. The Basel edition of 1578 to 1581 omitted tractate Avodah Zarah. The omitted passages were kept in printed lists of errata, the Chesronos Hashas, and many were later recovered from uncensored manuscripts in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a>.</p><p>Soncino printed the first tractates in 1483. Daniel Bomberg printed the first complete edition at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1520 to 1523, thought relatively free of censorship, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilna</a> edition of the 1830s onward is now by far the most common. Scholars regard its text as not uniformly reliable, and there is no modern critical edition of the whole.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Talmud&#8221; cites where it covers Manuscripts, Bomberg Talmud 1523, Froben Talmud 1578, Slavita Talmud 1795:</p><ul><li>Shana Strauch Schick, Intention in Talmudic law: between thought and deed (Brill, 2021)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilna</a> Talmud, in 25 volumes, remains the basic text of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> study in yeshivas and for scholars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, and its folio pagination is the convention of reference. Study methods keep changing: the Brisker method, now used in most yeshivas in some form, is sometimes criticised as a modern pilpul, and academic scholarship separates the text's strata and compares it with Zoroastrian texts to place it in its Iranian context, as Yaakov Elman and Shai Secunda do.</p><p>Translation has widened the audience. Adin Steinsaltz's modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> translation, begun in 1969 and finished in 2010, was followed by the Koren English edition in 42 volumes, the Schottenstein edition in 73 volumes completed in 2004, and Isidore Epstein's Soncino translation of 1935 to 1948. The William Davidson Talmud went online at Sefaria in 2017 under a Creative Commons licence. A Talmud for survivors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> was printed in the US zone of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from 1947 to 1950.</p><p>The Talmud has also been attacked, often by antisemitic writers, such as August Rohling in 1871. Scholars point out that many of the criticisms rest on falsehoods or on quotations taken out of context, and the Anti-Defamation League has described fabricated passages. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>, books titled Talmud, based on the writings of Marvin Tokayer rather than on the original, are widely read.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Talmud&#8221; cites where it covers Premodern estimates, Modern estimates, Early modern era:</p><ul><li>Monika Amsler, The Babylonian Talmud and late antique book culture (Cambridge University Press, 2023)</li><li>Shai Secunda, The Iranian Talmud: reading the Bavli in its Sasanian context (University of Pennsylvania Press, 2014)</li><li>Shai Secunda, The Talmud's red fence: menstrual impurity and difference in Babylonian Judaism and its Sasanian context (Oxford University Press, 2020)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Talmud is the Mishnah with the Gemara's commentary on it, compiled by rabbis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a> between 70 CE and the early seventh century. The Babylonian Talmud is the one usually meant, and in Rabbinic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> it is second in authority only to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>.</em></p>

